from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(10,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("laws-of-motion","Raju pushed a door near its hinge and then near its handle. \"The handle is easier,\" he said, and his teacher smiled: \"That is the moment of a force.\"",
 ["The turning effect of a force about a fixed point or axis is the moment of the force, or torque, equal to the force multiplied by the perpendicular distance. The principle of moments says that when a body is balanced, the clockwise moment equals the anticlockwise moment. Two equal and opposite forces form a couple.",
  "Newton's second law says that force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum, which gives F = ma, and a unit force produces an acceleration of 1 m/s^2 in a mass of 1 kg. Impulse is force multiplied by time. Newton's third law says every action has an equal and opposite reaction. The principle of conservation of linear momentum says the total momentum of a system stays constant if no external force acts, and the rocket uses it for propulsion.",
  "Newton's universal law of gravitation says every two bodies attract with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them, with G = 6.674 x 10^-11 N m^2 kg^-2. Acceleration due to gravity g changes with height and depth. Mass is the amount of matter and weight is the force of gravity. Apparent weight changes in a lift, and in free fall we feel weightlessness."],
 ["moment of force","Newton's second law","impulse","conservation of momentum","universal gravitation","weightlessness"],
 ("A force of 20 N acts at 0.5 m from the hinge. Find the moment.",["Moment = force x perpendicular distance.","20 x 0.5 = 10.","Moment = 10 N m."]),
 ("Mass and weight of a body are the same on the Moon.",["Mass stays the same.","Weight depends on g and is less on the Moon."]),
 ["Moment = force x distance.","F = ma.","Momentum is conserved without external force.","Gravitational force depends on masses and distance."],
 ["m|The turning effect of a force is its|moment|impulse|weight|It is torque.","t|Action and reaction are equal and opposite.|T|Newton's third law.","m|Impulse equals|force x time|mass x distance|g x m|It equals change of momentum.","t|Weight is constant everywhere.|F|It changes with g.","m|Rocket propulsion works on|conservation of momentum|Boyle's law|Ohm's law|Gas goes down, rocket goes up.","s|What is the value-symbol of the universal gravitational constant?|G|It is G.","m|The unit of force is|newton|joule|watt|1 N = 1 kg m/s^2.","t|In free fall a person feels weightless.|T|The apparent weight is zero."])
C("optics","Asha held a glass prism in sunlight and splashed a rainbow on the wall. \"White light is a mixture of colours,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Light travels in straight lines called rays. When it passes obliquely from one medium to another it bends, which is refraction. The first law of refraction says the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane, and the second is Snell's law: the ratio of sines of the angles is constant. The refractive index is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to its speed in the medium.",
  "A prism splits white light into seven colours, which is dispersion, because each colour has a different angle of refraction, with red bending least and violet most. Scattering of sunlight by atmospheric gases is Rayleigh scattering and explains the blue sky and red sunset.",
  "Lenses are convex or concave, and the lens formula 1/v - 1/u = 1/f, with magnification m = v/u and power P = 1/f in dioptres, describes the image. Convex lenses are used in magnifiers and cameras and concave lenses in correcting short sight. The human eye forms images on the retina. Defects such as myopia and hypermetropia are corrected using lenses. The microscope and telescope extend our sight."],
 ["refraction","Snell's law","dispersion","Rayleigh scattering","lens formula","defects of the eye"],
 ("A convex lens has focal length 0.25 m. Find its power.",["Power = 1 / f in metres.","1 / 0.25 = 4.","Power = +4 D."]),
 ("The sky is blue because it reflects the sea.",["Sky colour is due to scattering of sunlight.","Shorter blue wavelengths scatter more."]),
 ["Refraction obeys Snell's law.","A prism disperses white light.","Lens formula 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.","Myopia is corrected by a concave lens."],
 ["m|Splitting of white light is|dispersion|reflection|echo|Seen in a prism.","t|Violet light bends more than red.|T|Yes.","m|Power of a lens is measured in|dioptre|metre|ohm|P = 1/f.","t|Myopia is corrected with a convex lens.|F|A concave lens is used.","m|The blue sky is due to|Rayleigh scattering|refraction|dispersion|Blue scatters most.","s|What is the screen of the eye called?|retina|Images form on the retina.","m|Refractive index is the ratio of speeds in|vacuum and medium|air and water only|two mediums of any kind|c / v.","t|A convex lens can act as a magnifier.|T|Yes."])
C("thermal-physics","On a hot day Meena noticed that the iron rails of a railway track have small gaps. \"Metals expand,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Temperature is the degree of hotness of a body, and thermal energy flows from the hotter body to the colder. Heat is the energy that causes the sensation of warmth. The kelvin scale is the absolute scale, with K = C + 273, and two bodies in thermal equilibrium have the same temperature.",
  "On heating, solids, liquids and gases expand, and expansion is measured as linear, area and volume expansion with coefficients. Real gases follow the gas laws closely only at low pressure and high temperature.",
  "Boyle's law says that at constant temperature the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume, Charles's law says that at constant pressure volume is proportional to the absolute temperature, and Avogadro's law relates volume to the number of molecules. An ideal gas obeys PV = nRT, where R = 8.31 J per mol per K."],
 ["temperature","thermal equilibrium","expansion","Boyle's law","Charles's law","ideal gas equation"],
 ("A gas at 2 atm occupies 6 L. Find its volume at 4 atm and constant temperature.",["Boyle's law: P1 V1 = P2 V2.","2 x 6 = 4 x V2.","V2 = 3 L."]),
 ("Heat and temperature are the same.",["Heat is energy.","Temperature measures the degree of hotness."]),
 ["Heat flows from hot to cold.","K = C + 273.","Boyle: PV constant.","PV = nRT for ideal gas."],
 ["m|The absolute scale is the|kelvin scale|Celsius scale|Fahrenheit scale|Zero is absolute zero.","t|Boyle's law is about constant temperature.|T|Yes.","m|Charles's law relates volume to|absolute temperature|time|colour|At constant pressure.","t|Thermal energy flows from cold to hot bodies.|F|It flows from hot to cold.","m|The ideal gas equation is|PV = nRT|F = ma|V = IR|R = 8.31.","s|What is the value of R in J per mol per K, nearest tenth?|8.3;8.31|About 8.31.","m|Bodies at the same temperature are in|thermal equilibrium|collision|reaction|No heat flows.","t|Solids expand on heating.|T|Yes."])
C("electricity","Raju's mother pressed the mixer button and the motor hummed. \"Electric power at work,\" said his father.",
 ["Electric current is the rate of flow of charge, I = Q / t, with the unit ampere. Potential difference between two points is the work done in moving a unit positive charge from one point to the other, and its unit is the volt. A circuit needs components like cell, switch, bulb, ammeter and voltmeter.",
  "Ohm's law says that at constant temperature V = IR, where R is the resistance in ohms. Resistivity is the resistance of a conductor of unit length and unit cross-section, and conductance is the opposite of resistance. In series R = R1 + R2 + R3, and in parallel 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.",
  "Joule's law gives the heat produced as H = I^2 R t, used in heaters and bulbs. Electric power is P = VI with the unit watt, and electrical energy is measured in kilowatt hour. Domestic circuits use fuses and earthing, because overloading and short circuits cause danger."],
 ["electric current","potential difference","Ohm's law","resistivity","series and parallel","Joule's law"],
 ("A 4 ohm and a 6 ohm resistor are in series. Find the total resistance.",["In series, add resistances.","4 + 6 = 10.","Total R = 10 ohm."]),
 ("In parallel, the total resistance is more than the largest resistor.",["In parallel total resistance is less than the smallest.","More paths make flow easier."]),
 ["V = IR.","Series: add R.","Parallel: add reciprocals.","P = VI."],
 ["m|The unit of current is|ampere|volt|ohm|Charge per second.","t|Ohm's law is V = IR.|T|Yes.","m|Total resistance in series is the|sum|difference|product|R = R1 + R2.","t|A fuse protects a circuit from overload.|T|It melts and cuts the current.","m|Electric power is|VI|V/I|I/V|Unit is watt.","s|What unit measures electrical energy bills? Two words.|kilowatt hour;kilowatt-hour|Kilowatt hour.","m|Heat produced by current follows|Joule's law|Hooke's law|Boyle's law|H = I^2 R t.","t|In parallel the total resistance is smaller than any single resistor.|T|Yes."])
